A||||17||btoth||Calling all networking experts||||5||
Z||000000||btoth||10-07-2001||10:42 PM||||OK, I have a linksys router with my computer (win 2k) and my dad's on the network with DSL internet, static ip, all that jazz  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  The only ports I have opened up are 80 and 21, both pointing to my computer for my web and ftp servers.  Anyway, I wanted to know if any of you know how I can setup the firewall to allow access to a shared folder on my HD.  I know that ports 137-139 are file sharing ports and if I open them up all my shared folders are freely out on the internet, which is not what I want.  I was looking for a way to specifically point one port that I assign to a shared folder on my computer.  Any ideas?<P>Other question:  does anyone know what port is used for Virtual Private Networking?  I was trying to set my computer up for incomming VPN connections.||||reg||5
Z||000001||artherd||10-08-2001||12:09 AM||||windows filesharing cannot be made secure, just use the web/ftp outside your firewall   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000002||8Ball||10-08-2001||12:21 AM||||Two words.<P>Red Hat  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JSocha||10-08-2001||12:23 AM||||You may need to set up a NAT relationship within your firewall with a static IP (provided by your ISP) outside with an internal IP inside.<P>A Virtual Private Network (VPN) may work as well.  <P>I'm drugged out right now on muscle relaxants so I am not reading or comprehending very well, let alone thinking.  Just got back from a late night chiropractor appointment.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||10-08-2001||03:36 AM||||To my knowledge you can't get that kind of granularity on a Linksys.  You might be able to restrict access through the linksys to those ports from a single IP address (since your dad has a static IP) to the single IP address of your internal machine.  One thing you won't be able to do is to restrict access to a specific folder or folders; that's a function of the file server to control.  You should also make sure that directed broadcasts are disabled if possible (a good idea anyways).<P>As to the comment re: Red Hat, Linux (like Solaris for example) is pretty insecure right out of the box. You have to disable some services and reconfigure or install others (even with a 'high security' installation) to have a reasonably secure box.  This also extends to the Samba installation; earlier versions are vulnerable to remote root exploits and an improperly configured smb.conf leads to insecure volume shares. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>OK, I have a linksys router with my computer (win 2k) and my dad's on the network with DSL internet, static ip, all that jazz   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  The only ports I have opened up are 80 and 21, both pointing to my computer for my web and ftp servers.  Anyway, I wanted to know if any of you know how I can setup the firewall to allow access to a shared folder on my HD.  I know that ports 137-139 are file sharing ports and if I open them up all my shared folders are freely out on the internet, which is not what I want.  I was looking for a way to specifically point one port that I assign to a shared folder on my computer.  Any ideas?<P>Other question:  does anyone know what port is used for Virtual Private Networking?  I was trying to set my computer up for incomming VPN connections.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||10-08-2001||05:21 AM||||Just enable file/printer sharing and install something like ZoneAlarm on your PC. ZoneAlarm will only allow the files to be shared with your local network.||||reg||1
Z||000006||grinthock||10-08-2001||10:21 AM||||Whoa guys there is an EASIER way...<P>For simplicity just install IPX/SPX on your's and your dad's machine, then you can talk no problems.  I am running TCP/IP all blocked here and then IPX for the sharing.  It's better anyway way faster than TCP/IP on file trans.||||reg||1
Z||000007||thomas_l||10-08-2001||10:37 AM||||Why would you want Windows file sharing when you are already running an FTP server? You could either put a pointer (shortcut) on your FTP server or run another FTP server on a weird port if you want to lock it down.||||reg||5
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||10-08-2001||11:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>For simplicity just install IPX/SPX on your's and your dad's machine, then you can talk no problems.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The problem with installing IPX/SPX is that this will create <B>a lot</B> of extra traffic on your local net. Especially if you play games over the internet, you'll notice a lot of short interruptions which are caused by the IPX/SPX protocol searching for other computers.||||reg||1
Z||000009||grinthock||10-08-2001||01:24 PM||||Hmmm Cliff, I am not sure on that, it's not going to create THAT much more traffic I wouldn't think, my network is good with the IPX running.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cliff Pennock||10-08-2001||02:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>Hmmm Cliff, I am not sure on that, it's not going to create THAT much more traffic I wouldn't think, my network is good with the IPX running.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A single computer will not create much traffic, but all the computers on the network together will create a lot of extra traffic. The problem is that the TCP/IP protocol suffers from this.<P>There's no reason whatsoever to have two different protocols running next to each other.||||reg||1
Z||000011||falconhulk||10-08-2001||03:01 PM||||share the folders and put a $ after the names.  This makes the folders hidden.  Shut your PC down when not in use.  Assign permissons to those folders to specific accounts instead of allowing access to everyone.  Being paranoid but with your case I would not worry.  Windows file sharing on 9x systems cannot be made secure 2k is based on NT.  Thousands of corporations rely on this server everyday.  As long as you secure the folders with standard procedures there is nothing to worry about.  BTW I am a NT/2000 administrator.  <p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 10-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||84Bill||10-08-2001||03:09 PM||||If your running IIS you can add a "virtual directory" that points to a folder on your hard drive, you can the edit the properties on that folder to allow "directory browsing" and change the directory security from annonomus to clear text and uncheck the nt authentication( bottom option). this will prompt for a user ID and password that you can setup in user manager.<P>My server is down right now but you can browse this folder as an example. If I change the permissions I can prompt for a user ID and Password<BR> <A HREF="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/stuff" TARGET=_blank>http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/stuff</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 10-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||LarryB||10-08-2001||03:25 PM||||<BR> <A HREF="http://www.zdnet.com/zdnn/stories/news/0,4586,2814546,00.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.zdnet.com/zdnn/stories/news/0,4586,2814546,00.html</A> ||||reg||11
Z||000014||falconhulk||10-08-2001||03:40 PM||||yes IIS is vulnarable.  That is if you do not keep up on the patches.||||reg||1
Z||000015||ssalyers||10-08-2001||04:05 PM||||Port 1723 is for VPN connections, but on 2000 there is at least on other port that it authenticates throught (which I think is 49, but not sure).  RedHat IS the way to go though and you can use IT as the router.<P>--Shawn||||reg||3
Z||000016||falconhulk||10-09-2001||01:28 PM||||Why would you want to use it as the router if you already have a hardware router???||||reg||1
Z||000017||grinthock||10-09-2001||10:29 PM||||Remembering!! <P>Sorry I am a dufus, the reason that here at home I couldn't use TCP/IP for file sharing IS...  My ISP for some reason when they give IP's to my machines they ALL get IP's on DIFFERENT SUBNETS!!  Go figure and because of that when MAchien A tries to talk to Machine B, it goes through the default next hop ROUTER!!!  Which is my ISP!  And my cable modem BLOCKS Netbios, hence I need to use IPX here.  Unless I wanna do a whole lot of other stuff I am too lazy to do||||reg||1
